sequenceDiagram
  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake): resume a sleeping development container, or start one that was stopped. A request to its Route admits the same kind through the runtime activation endpoint of docs/reference/app-dev-runtime-hibernation.md#wake.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:wake
  %% kind: instance.wake
  autonumber
  %% lane CL icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CL as Caller<br/>orbit · Caddy
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container
  CL->>GW: instance:wake INSTANCE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:wake INSTANCE`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--json]`
  %%> Callers:
  %%> - the command
  %%> - Caddy on the Instance's Node, when a request finds the asleep marker: `GET runtime-activations/app-instance/ID`
  %%> - planned: a Conn device that opens the T3 server, and the task flow before a turn on an attached Instance
  %%> - a deployment of a sleeping default starts it in its own run instead
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the caller is the Instance's Node or has node access to it
  Note over GW: Start or join the wake<br/>one per Instance
  %%> When:
  %%> - an active development container that is not a nested-fleet Instance
  %%> - a clone of a nested-fleet default takes its start instead: see instance/start.mmd
  %%> Result:
  %%> - an open wake of the Instance is joined: a second caller gets the same Operation, Caddy the same progress page
  %%> - after a failed wake, Caddy gets the failure page with Try again until a retry or the command starts a new wake
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - no container, a nested-fleet Instance or other lifecycle work starts no run
  opt the last sleep froze the container
    %% path: resume
    GW->>N: Write the wake request<br/>agent · hibernation.marker
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the Operation is recorded without its run; the Node calls run after its commit
    %%> - the frozen agent's `hibernation.marker` writes `/dev/shm/orbit/hibernation/app-instance-<id>.wake`; only root writes in that folder
    %%> - the app-dev role's script, started by a systemd path unit, acts only on `instance-<id>` of `orbit-instances` found Frozen with its asleep marker and a request under 1 s old: `incus resume`, `memory.swap.max` 0, FrankenPHP until it answers below 500 (1.5 s at most), then it removes the marker and writes `app-instance-<id>.resumed`
    %%> - the Gateway reads the request folder and the marker with `hibernation.times` until the resume shows, within 3 s
    %%> Why:
    %%> - measured on a frozen clone: the resume takes 0.02 s and the web probe 0.4 s, but the run's start-up and playbook took 8-13 s of the 9-19 s a visitor waited
    %%> - the agent stays frozen (ADR 0253): two of its existing commands carry the request and the answer
    %%> Result:
    %%> - resumed: the Operation succeeds here with `wake: resumed`, `trigger_seconds` and `confirm_seconds`; `power_state` is `running`; Conn's renewal below runs as after a run
    %%> - Caddy gets 307 to the same path: the browser sends the request again, with its method and body, and gets the app
    %%> - an API caller gets 202 with the ended Operation
    %%> - a caller who joins this wake waits for it up to the bound, then gets the same answer
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - no script took the request in 0.6 s, the script declined (another state, FrankenPHP silent), an agent call or the Gateway failed, or the bound passed: the Gateway removes the request, and the same Operation starts the run below
    %%> - a Gateway worker that dies here: a delayed start runs the same Operation's run 15 s later
    %%> - a cancel during the resume keeps the Operation cancelled
  end
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-wake
  GW-->>CL: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID · progress page
  SW->>N: Wake the container<br/>Ansible · tags start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Frozen: `incus resume`, `memory.swap.max` back to 0, FrankenPHP gets 10 tries 1 s apart; nothing boots and nothing converges, `wake: resumed`
  %%> - Frozen and FrankenPHP silent: `incus stop --force`, then the boot below, `wake: restarted_after_resume`
  %%> - Stopped (after a host reboot or a sleep that fell back to a stop): the boot below, `wake: started`
  %%> - Running (after a reboot with `boot.autostart`): no start and no resume, `wake: already_running`
  %%> - a Frozen container without the marker is resumed; the result says `marker_found: false`
  %%> - boot: `incus start`; nftables, T3, FrankenPHP and the enabled Process units start with the container
  %%> - waits until each desired-running unit is active, then until FrankenPHP answers, else `instance.web_unready`
  %%> - a 5xx answer gets up to 5 s to clear; an app that answers 5xx by itself is then served as it is
  %%> - removes the asleep marker, so Caddy sends the next request to the app
  %%> - then waits up to 30 s for T3's environment endpoint in a clone, else `instance.t3_unready`
  %%> Why:
  %%> - one playbook: a second Ansible start costs about 4 s on the visitor's path
  %%> - the visitor waits for the app, not for T3; T3 gates only the Operation's end
  %%> - unit enablement holds each Process's desired state, so a stopped Process stays stopped
  %%> When:
  %%> - the marker part runs only in a start whose intent names the marker; a deployment's start has none
  alt the run passes
    %% path: main
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
    Note over GW: Record awake
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `running`; the Conn layer shows the T3 server awake
    %%> - the result names the path, `marker_found`, and a resume's `resume_seconds` and `web_probe_seconds`
    %%> - the progress page loads the original path
    opt the Gateway session ends within 7 days or has ended
      Note over GW: Admit the renewal<br/>conn.environment_renew
      %%> When:
      %%> - after the wake's transaction; it never fails the wake
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - see conn/environment-renew.mmd; the run needs no old session, so a session that ended during the sleep renews too
    end
  else T3 does not answer
    %% path: t3-unready
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>instance.t3_unready
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Operation fails; the marker is gone and the Route serves the app
    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `running`, because the container runs
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - the same command; a start of a running container changes nothing
  else a step fails before the app answers
    %% path: failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the asleep marker stays; the Route shows the failure page with Try again
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - Try again or the same command; a start of a running container changes nothing
  end
  loop until terminal
    CL->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CL: Status and result
  end
